    {
      "id": "kr-N0GR",
      "step": 0,
      "order": 1,
      "family": "plate",
      "title": "Plate: green (old), white or yellow (new)",
      "text": [
        "South Korea uses a variety of different licence plates.",
        "- Pre-2006 plates are short and **green**. These will still show up in the street view coverage.",
        "- Post-2006 plates are **white** for private vehicles, and **yellow** for commercial vehicles. These can be long and short."
      ],
      "tags": [],
      "images": [
        {
          "id": "de2accd09ba4",
          "src": "https://www.plonkit.net/images/south-korea/licen_plae.png",
          "path": "kr/de2accd09ba4.webp",
          "width": 1000,
          "height": 464,
          "role": "comparison",
          "caption": "South Korean plates: green (old), white and yellow (new)"
        }
      ],
      "locations": [],
      "lookalikes": [],
      "credit": {
        "source": "Plonk It",
        "url": "https://www.plonkit.net/south-korea",
        "licence": "CC BY-NC-SA 4.0"
      },
      "summary": "Plates from before 2006 are short and green and still show up in coverage. Newer ones are white for private cars and yellow for commercial vehicles, short or long."
    },
    {
      "id": "kr-WNFu",
      "step": 0,
      "order": 2,
      "family": "coverage",
      "title": "Google car: Gen 3, black, white or silver",
      "text": [
        "Almost all road coverage in South Korea is made with a **Generation 3** camera.",
        "In most, but not all, of the coverage, the back of the Google car is visible. You can see three colours: **black, white** and **silver**. The car never has an antenna."
      ],
